 \f1\fs22 Scientists would say yes. Doctors would say yes, and would say it publicly, in a manner that would make anyone who disagreed sound like a murderer. Government ministers would leak, from the ordinary human need to be the first at a dinner party to know something.\
 Hardy wanted an answer that would stand up, from people with no stake in the outcome, and no standing to be quoted.\
-So he looked at his finance people, and from the finance department he looked at the juniors. There were accountants, economists, finance advisers and a woman from Treasury Modelling whose job title ran to fourteen words and described, in essence, the decorating of graphs. They were people who spent their days building models nobody read, of futures nobody intended to have, for reports that existed so that a decision already taken could be described afterwards as evidence-based. They were good at it. They were good at it the way a well-made hinge is good: silent, and without any expectation of being looked at.\
+So he looked at his finance people, and from the finance department he looked at the juniors. There were accountants, economists, finance advisers and a woman from Treasury Modelling whose job title ran to fourteen words and described, in essence, the decorating of graphs. They were people who spent their days building models nobody read, of futures nobody intended to have, for reports that existed so that a decision already taken could be described afterwards as evidence-based. They were good at it. They were good at it the way a well-made hinge is good: silent, frictionless, and without any expectation of being looked at.\
 He had a dozen of them collected in the dark of night, and not told where they were going.\
 They were going to the office tower in the Pale, though nobody called it that back then; it was simply the part of the inner city where Hardy had labs. The lift went down for a long time. It went down long enough that two of the juniors got the giggles, and then stopped, as people do when a lift keeps going without a light on the panel changing.\
 The corridor went on for a long time too; at a cross-roads, Hardy stopped and read a sign. He stood in front of it in a suit worth more than the annual maintenance budget of the level he was standing on, in a building he owned outright. He read the sign twice then went left.\
