-\f3\fs26 [ THE AUDIOSCAPE ]\
-- The heavy, synthetic rockabilly rhythm of the emancipated Elvis clones fills the room. \
-- The music provides a subtle world-building anchor: a reminder of what corporations \
-  like Grayling and Hardy do to genetic material when they own it.\
-\
-[ THE MECHANICAL TRIUMPH ]\
-- Sarah is on the exercise bike, her legs moving in a blur. \
-- Janine stands by the door with her coat on, watching her daughter's calves and thighs. \
-- The muscle definition is smooth, dense, and firm. The wasting has vanished. \
-\
-[ THE CONVERSATION ]\
-- Sarah looks up, shouting over the music about her plans for a real bike in the park.\
-- Janine steps in to check her vitals one last time under the guise of fixing her hair or \
-  wiping away sweat. She touches the muscle. It is tight and resilient.\
-- Janine tells her she won't need any more "vitamin shots." The treatment is over. \
-  The loophole worked. Sarah is self-sustaining.\
-\
-[ THE SEPARATION ]\
-- Janine leaves for her shift at the Pale, carrying the deep relief of a mother who \
-  believes the danger is behind them. \
-- Cut to the Pale: The heavy lock security bulkheads slam down on Hardy\'92s orders. \
-  Janine is sealed inside the tomb, while Sarah remains at home, pedaling toward a \
-  freedom she doesn't know she has just lost.\
