-\f0\i0 \strokec2 \
-
-\f4\i \strokec2 Sarah was pedaling to the beat. Her knees rose and fell in perfect, fluid lines\'97no hitch in the hip, no telltale sway of a collapsing pelvic girdle. For three years, Janine had watched her daughter\'92s legs slowly turn to water. Now, the quadriceps pulled taut against the skin, dense and re-sculpted.
-\f0\i0 \strokec2 \
-
-\f4\i \strokec2 \'93Next week, Mom!\'94 Sarah yelled over the vocals, her face flushed with fresh micro-circulation but her breathing entirely steady. \'93The real one. You promised we\'92d go to the park by the river.\'94
-\f0\i0 \strokec2 \
-
-\f4\i \strokec2 Janine walked over, reaching out to catch a strand of her daughter's hair. Her fingers brushed the top of Sarah's shoulder. The deltoid muscle underneath was firm, pressing back with a spring-like resilience. It had been twenty days since the last high-concentration micro-injection. If the virus was still episomal, the histone methylation should have throttled it by Tuesday. Sarah should have been sluggish. She should have been failing.
-\f0\i0 \strokec2 \
