+Mum, everyone’s life is a test run.
+The root of all evil is not the decisions we make, it’s the information available.
+
+———
+“And I’d need to be on it forever.”
+Janine closed her eyes until she’d aligned herself to what she’d already committed to. “I can give you a short course, higher dosage, over just a few weeks.”
+“That’s it?”
+“Yes.” There was no way Janine could appropriate the drug over an extended period. Getting her hands on higher quantities for a short period of time was more… pragmatic. “The normal dose is designed to spread through the body,” Janine said. “What I’m talking about is putting a much higher concentration exactly where you need it.”
+Janine had rehearsed the next bit—in the car, in the shower, at three in the morning, and once, briefly while standing in front of the fridge with the door open, until it beeped with impatience.
+“I believe your condition—”
+“My Sarco… glycan… opathy,” Pin Cushion said, mimicking her six-year-old self.
+“Well, yes… muscular dystrophies in general. They will allow dosages that would normally be toxic.”
+“Toxic, as in… deadly.”
+“Potentially.”
+Pin Cushion stared at her.
+Janine pushed on. “The toxicity models assume the dose is circulating through the whole body. I’m talking about injecting a much higher concentration directly into damaged muscle. Your muscles are continually being damaged and repaired. TPlus is extraordinarily effective in damaged tissue.”
+Pin Cushion tucked one leg beneath herself. “And then I’m cured?”
